From 2808eb18b67ffce1c2bc5093d50052655c72a669 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: n0madic <800462+n0madic@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 11:10:15 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Cut a Ghidra map function with a far chunk at the next
 function

The Ghidra function CSV gives End as the highest address of a function's
body. A body with a far chunk, typically a shared tail reached by a jump,
therefore spans every function in between, and the recompiler decoded the
whole span with an entry at every address: in Soulcalibur III
FUN_003b6428 has Size 32 but End 0x3D2D08 and came out as 25,770
instructions. 18 such functions added 0.31M instructions, and the six
generated units holding the largest ones took 4 to 61 minutes each to
compile.

A body never runs into the start of another function, so when a span
exceeds Size, end it at the next function start of the map (labels such
as entry_ and caseD_ do not count). Bodies with only small holes (2,907
in Soulcalibur III) have no function start in their span and keep their
End.

The long span also stood in for what Ghidra missed in it, so two things
are kept explicitly. Runs of non-zero words in the cut-away part that no
map entry covers become functions (code reached only through pointers,
e.g. 0x40abd8, called by the ADX reader). A direct branch, jump or call
from anywhere into the middle of another function in a cut span gets an
entry there (e.g. JAL 0x406928 from 0x4069c8). Without either, the game
stopped on a missing target and showed a black screen.
